            "text": "Makes a tensor of random samples from the BETA distribution: values between 0 and 1 in a shape set by two numbers.\n\nIt is the most flexible distribution on 0..1. Both parameters 1 give a flat line; both above 1 a hump; both below 1 a U, piled up at the two ends; unequal values lean the shape towards one end. Use it for random proportions, probabilities, mix amounts and positions along a line, wherever the result must stay inside 0..1 but should not be flat.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.dist.beta <dim 0: int> <dim 1: int> ...\nWhole numbers give the shape of each batch of samples; with none\nthe node sends one sample at a time.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.dist.beta 64 64\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin (button):\nThe small button at the top left of the node. Click it, or send it anything, for a fresh tensor of samples. Only this inlet makes the node send.\n\nshape (text, also an inlet):\nThe size of the tensor of samples, as whole numbers: [64, 64] is a 64 by 64 grid, [1000] a row of a thousand. Numbers typed after the node name set it (t.dist.beta 64 64); with none it is [1], a single sample. A list or tensor of numbers, or a single number, sent into the inlet works too. A new shape does not send by itself - the next click uses it.\n\nalpha (drag float, also an inlet):\nPulls the samples towards 1. Must be above 0. Default 0.5.\n\nbeta (drag float, also an inlet):\nPulls the samples towards 0. Must be above 0. Default 0.5.\n\nThe mean is alpha / (alpha + beta); the larger both are, the narrower the hump around it. (In torch's terms alpha is concentration1 and beta concentration0.)\n\nEach parameter is a drag float on the node and also an inlet. Setting one rebuilds the distribution but does NOT send; the next click samples from it. In the demo each slider goes through 't bang float', which passes the number to the parameter first and THEN clicks the node, so dragging a slider re-samples at once. A parameter that must be above 0 has a drag float that stops at 0.000001. A value the distribution cannot take (see each parameter) - 0 or below sent into such an inlet, say - prints a message in the console, and the node goes on sampling with the last parameters that worked.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrandom tensor:\nA float32 tensor on the cpu, in the shape set, filled with fresh independent samples on every click.\n\nTHE HISTOGRAM:\nt.histc divides the range from its min to its max into equal bins and counts how many of the 4096 samples land in each; the bar plot draws the counts, the first bin at the left. Its shape is the shape of the distribution. Samples outside min..max are not counted at all.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nload_bang draws the first 4096 samples when the page opens; click the node's own button for more. At the defaults (0.5 and 0.5) the heat_map (0 dark, 1 bright) is nearly all very dark and very bright cells, and the histogram is a U: tall bars at both ends, low in the middle (the end bars can run off the top of the plot). Drag both to 1 for a flat line, both to 5 for a hump in the middle, then make one larger than the other and the hump slides towards that end.\n\nRELATED:\nt.dist.kumaraswamy makes very similar shapes. t.dist.uniform is beta with both 1. t.dist.continuous_bernoulli is another 0..1 shape with a single parameter."
        },
